## Recommendation: Use `System.IO.Abstractions`

For `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists`, the best approach is the established **System.IO.Abstractions** NuGet package — no need to hand-roll a wrapper.

### 1. Install the package

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
// MockFileSystem also supports Directory.Exists — directories are
// inferred from file paths or created explicitly.
mockFs.Directory.CreateDirectory("/data/logs");

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Load("/data/config.json"));
Assert.True(manager.DirectoryExists("/data/logs"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?**
- Battle-tested, complete API surface (covers `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream`, etc.)
- `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem out of the box — no mocking framework needed
- Community standard; well-maintained

Once you've added the package and DI registration, the next step is migrating existing call sites. If you'd like help bulk-replacing the static calls with `IFileSystem` usage across your project, just ask.
